WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:05.998
Figure nineteen. Shorts, by Keelson.

00:07.800 --> 00:34.116
Every short in this series came off one production line. A script names the scenes and the narration. Then the same steps run every time: voice the lines, draw the boards, compose the cut, and check it. For the first eighteen figures, those steps ran by hand. Steps like that are a recipe, so now they're a Keelson workflow. Whether the cut is any good is a judgment, so that part is a gate.

00:35.116 --> 00:39.144
This is figure sixteen's script. Change one line of narration.

00:42.016 --> 00:45.294
One word changed, one added. That's the whole edit.

00:45.416 --> 00:47.308
Then hand the short to the workflow.

00:50.516 --> 00:53.144
It voices only the line that changed.

00:53.916 --> 00:58.856
Then it draws the boards and composes the cut, the same steps every time.

01:00.216 --> 01:03.908
Composing takes a few minutes. Then QA checks the cut.

01:07.416 --> 01:27.012
A check can measure a cut: its length, its loudness, any long silence. It can't see a cursor in the wrong place, or a caption over the answer. So QA writes a review page, a contact sheet of nine frames from across the cut, the numbers, and the script, and the run waits on it.

01:28.112 --> 01:31.028
Paused, with the contact sheet open for review.

01:34.250 --> 01:37.350
The numbers, and every line of the script.

01:40.817 --> 01:42.677
It looks right. Approve.

01:43.866 --> 01:48.886
The cut is staged for upload. The upload itself stays a person's job.

01:50.966 --> 02:02.148
From here on, that's how a figure gets cut: fixed steps in a graph, a person wherever the call is judgment, and nothing leaving without a yes. It's the way Keelson runs any job.

02:02.748 --> 02:04.800
This figure was cut by it too.

02:07.500 --> 02:11.608
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.
